  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 111 PM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery shows\u00a0a shortwave trough over \nsouthern California bringing widespread clouds and scattered rain \nshowers to the Desert Southwest. Ahead of this, there is a dry \nslot which will result in mostly clear skies for northeast \nColorado through this evening. High clouds then increase late \ntonight and persist through Saturday morning. At the surface, the \npattern changes very little as a surface trough remains near the \nbase of the foothills, which will help keep low level moisture in \nplace over northeast Colorado. This will lead to another round of \nfog late tonight and Saturday morning. The mid and high clouds \nassociated with the shortwave trough tracking east-northeast \nacross the intermountain west will shift east of the area midday \nSaturday. Mostly sunny skies are expected during the afternoon, \nhelping to push temperatures into the upper 50s to mid 60s across \nnortheast Colorado.\u00a0\n\nSouthwest flow aloft will increase during the day Sunday as two \nsystems off to the west take aim at the region. The first one \nsystem, a shortwave trough moves across southern California and \ntracks east-northeast Sunday and Monday. This system will run \nahead of the second system on Sunday, carrying subtropical \nmoisture with it and, like many of the recent systems, bring \nmoderate to heavy rain to the Desert Southwest and Four Corners \nRegion. For north central and northeast Colorado, this has \nresulted in scattered showers over the mountains and just cloudy \nskies for northeast Colorado. The second system tracks eastward \nacross Oregon and northern California Sunday and then moves across\nColorado Monday. The best chance for rain and mountain snow will \ncome late Sunday night and Monday as the main trough moves \noverhead. The snow level starts off high, above 11,000 feet but \nquickly lowers to 8,500 to 9,000 feet by Monday morning. Snow is \nexpected to continue into Monday night when flow aloft turns \nnorth-northwesterly, a somewhat favorable direction for orographic\nlift across the north central mountains. Temperatures will be \ncool Monday due to the overhead trough and cloud cover. Northerly \nflow behind the trough Tuesday will keep temperatures cool with \nhighs in the 50s with dry conditions.\u00a0A few earlier model runs \ncombined these two systems over the Central Rockies producing a \nstrong storm system, but this scenario now looks very unlikely.\u00a0\n\nThe broad troughiness shifts east of the area sometime late \nTuesday/early Wednesday with ridging replacing\u00a0it, most likely for\nWednesday. We expect to see a bump up in temperatures with dry \nconditions. The ridging is expected to progress east of the area \non Thursday. What replaces it is still unclear. The next upper \nlevel trough moves over the western states on\u00a0Thursday. Still very\nlittle model agreement on the placement, strength, and track of \nthe upper level trough for Thursday and Friday. For the forecast, \ntemperatures should be near normal. We will have some low PoPs in \nthe mountains and keep the plains dry for now.",
